            <a href="/">Home</a>
            <span>›</span>
            <a href="/seo">SEO</a>
            <span>›</span>
            <a href="/seo/backlinks">Link Building</a>
            <span>›</span>
            <span>UK Posting Sites</span>
          </div>

          <span class="card-icon pillar-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-list-check" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Link building</p>
          <h1>{title}</h1>
          <p class="pillar-intro">{intro}</p>
          <PageByline />
          <figure class="posting-feature">
            <img src="/images/posting/uk-posting-sites.png" width="1200" height="630" alt={title} loading="eager" />
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Here is the thing about a list like this: 100 sites is about 90 more than you need.</p>

            <p>You are not going to pitch all of them, and you should not try. The goal is not "get a link from every UK site with a high Domain Rating". The goal is a handful of relevant placements on sites your actual customers read.</p>

            <p>This list is the raw material. Below the table you will find how to filter it to a shortlist, write a pitch that gets a reply, and avoid the mistakes that get you ignored or blocked.</p>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <p>Every site in the table has five numbers next to it. Here is how to read them, and how much each one actually matters.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR; if the two disagree, trust neither blindly</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits to the site per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> a DR 90 site in a niche unrelated to yours does almost nothing for you. A DR 65 site your buyers genuinely read is worth chasing. Sort by relevance first. Use the metrics to break ties.</p></div>

            <h2 id="shortlist">How to pick your shortlist</h2>
            <p>Work down the table in this order:</p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Relevance.</strong> Cross out every site with nothing to do with your industry or audience. For most businesses that removes 70 to 80 of the 100.</li>
              <li><strong>Real traffic.</strong> Of what is left, keep the sites with meaningful monthly traffic. A link on a page nobody visits is a vanity link.</li>
              <li><strong>Contribution policy.</strong> Check each remaining site for a "write for us", "contribute" or "editorial guidelines" page. Several national titles near the top only take PR pitches, not open guest posts.</li>
              <li><strong>Reachability.</strong> Can you find a named editor or a clear submission route? A faceless contact form usually means a low reply rate.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>You should end with 8 to 15 genuine targets. That is your list.</p>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 UK sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority and URL Rating. Every domain here was checked live in September 2026; dead and parked sites were removed. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time, so re-check the two or three you plan to pitch.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that gets a reply</h2>
            <p>Most guest-post pitches fail for the same reason. They lead with the ask instead of the idea.</p>
            <p>Do this instead:</p>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Read three of their recent articles.</strong> Note the angle, the length and the tone.</li>
              <li><strong>Find the gap.</strong> What have they not covered, or covered badly, that you can genuinely add to?</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the specific piece,</strong> not "I would like to write for you". Give the working headline, a two-line summary, and why their readers would care.</li>
              <li><strong>Prove you can write.</strong> Link to one article you are proud of. One, not five.</li>
              <li><strong>Keep it short.</strong> Four or five sentences. Editors skim.</li>
              <li><strong>Follow up once,</strong> about a week later. Then move on.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would this pitch still make sense if you removed the link you want entirely? If yes, send it. If the whole email only exists to get a backlink, the editor can tell.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Send the same pitch to all 100 sites. Editors talk, and tools flag it.</li>
              <li>Pay for a "guest post" that is really a link placement, without a <code>sponsored</code> tag. That is a paid link and it is a risk.</li>
              <li>Stuff the post with exact-match anchor text pointing at your money pages.</li>
              <li>Submit thin or AI-spun filler. A bad guest post damages your brand on someone else's site.</li>
              <li>Chase Domain Rating at the expense of relevance. It is the number one mistake people make with lists like this.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 id="expect">What to realistically expect</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Question</th><th>Realistic answer</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Reply rate on a good, personalised pitch</td><td>15 to 30 percent</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Time from pitch to published</td><td>2 to 8 weeks</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Links from the national news sites at the top</td><td>Usually only through digital PR or a genuine expert-comment request</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Where most achievable placements are</td><td>The mid-list trade, business and industry sites</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> five to ten relevant, editorial placements over a few months will do more for your rankings than fifty low-quality ones, and carry none of the risk. Use this list to find those five to ten.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
              {toc.map((item) => (
                <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq">
        <Faq items={faqs} />
      </div>

      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="cta-panel" style="text-align: center; max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto;">
            <h2>Want us to run the outreach?</h2>
            <p>
              We build the shortlist, write the pitches and the posts, and place them on relevant
              UK sites with real editorial standards. No link farms, no risk.
            </p>
            <div class="hero-actions" style="justify-content: center; margin-top: 0;">
              <a href="/services" class="button">View services</a>
              <a
                href={whatsappUrl("Hi SERP Mentor, I'd like help with UK guest posting and link building.")}
                class="button-secondary"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                <i class="fa-brands fa-whatsapp" aria-hidden="true"></i> WhatsApp
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <SiteFooter />
  </body>
</html>
